<script>
import getSummarySource from 'crm/src/reports/getSummary.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import PipelineSummary from 'crm/src/reports/lib/PipelineSummary.abide'
import pipelineSummarySource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/PipelineSummary.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import pipelineSummaryTestSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/PipelineSummary.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A test holds the app with `createApp`">
        <Example
            id="app-fetch"
            app="crm"
            title="`createApp()` — the app a test holds, with no port"
            summary="The reports page opens on a summary of the pipeline. Its test asks the app for the same handler the tiles read, as a request, and reads the answer the wire would carry."
            files={{
                'src/reports/lib/PipelineSummary.abide': pipelineSummarySource,
                'src/reports/getSummary.handler.ts': getSummarySource,
                'src/reports/lib/PipelineSummary.test.ts': pipelineSummaryTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <PipelineSummary />
        </Example>

        <p><code>createApp</code> builds the app from the directory it runs in, the way
            <code>abide start</code> does, and binds no port. Run <code>bun test</code> from the app's
            own directory. <code>app.fetch</code> takes a <code>Request</code> through everything a
            served one goes through: the middleware, the argument checks, the headers and the status a
            refusal carries.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>App</code></td><td>what <code>createApp</code> hands back, to a test, a script or a host</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>App.fetch</code></td><td>one request, through the whole app, in this process, as the <code>principal</code> and the <code>caller</code> you give it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>App.run</code></td><td>runs a function inside one request's scope, so two reads of one key load once, signed in as the <code>principal</code> claims you give it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>App.listen</code></td><td>binds the socket, for a test that needs a real <code>WebSocket</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>App.stop</code></td><td>stops the app: every <code>onStop</code>, wrapped around draining what is in flight</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>App[Symbol.asyncDispose]</code></td><td><code>stop</code>, under the name <code>await using</code> calls</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Hold the app with <code>await using</code> and it stops when the test ends, a failed one
            included. An app nobody stops leaves open whatever <code>onStart</code> opened, and the
            suite hangs with no report at all.</p>

        <p>A <code>.test.ts</code> has no address, so a test sits beside the file it tests and is
            never served.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
